e annotated v1.0.0 resolves to release_commit. Publish run 41339906440, conformance 41339904670 and proofs 41339904703 succeeded for exactly that commit. Both public PyPI files exactly equal dist artifact 13097191042 of publish run; all three installed wheel modules are byte-identical to impl/ at the tag.","resume":"46 PASS, 0 FAIL, 3 UNRUN; all resume mutants killed; Python/Rust/rich differentials agree; WebAssembly/native 60/60 agree and worker controls pass.","sdist_sha256":"7878e718f28ed932aa6d40205898d31909cc4baf701970d35b969d448fb13fd0","unrun":["N05 host atomic on-disk publication","N15 host memo cache","N27 Warrant attestations/signature trust"],"wheel_sha256":"36d3354d47c7657237038549513b6c46a35e48ca35072a672de76cccd2f7f7b7"},"reason":"sigma-resume section 5 defines a cycle witness as canonical JSON with exact fields and in-domain integers, and requires malformed witnesses to be refused before any replay. sigma_provider.verify_cycle calls ordinary json.loads on incoming bytes. It accepts noncanonical whitespace and silently discards duplicate names before validating the resulting dictionary. A duplicate cycle_action_count with an initial false value and final integer 1 therefore reaches replay as though the offending encoding never existed. The same holds at M and in the public 1.0.0 wheel. Claim witnesses operate on dictionaries, so the signed checker acceptances and the green suites do not exercise this raw-byte boundary. This is a conformance counterexample in a published normative interface, not a claim that publication failed.","release_commit":"321504a02c0d0a87eeb6b5dd4760a9b22a6d53af","remediation":"Validate raw cycle-witness JSON using duplicate-aware parsing, strict integer types and canonical byte equality before any replay, add wire-level provider regressions including installed-artifact checks, and publish corrected bytes under a fresh version rather than moving v1.0.0.","reproduce_commands":["python3 -m venv <work>/installed","<work>/installed/